In message <MPG.1beeaae3de3d4ec098a250@216.168.3.44>, Rast wrote:
..
> Haven't we learned by now that high score is simply a function of how much 
> time the player spends killing monsters before ascending?  (see last year's 
> tournament, and the one before that, and the one before that...)

Yup; we have.  ;-)

(You may notice that the Highest Score trophy is no longer the big kahuna.)

We left the score-based stuff this way for this folks who cannot reliably
ascend.  We don't want to emphasize the score too much, since we all agree it's
not a particularly menaingful measure of skill, but it _does_ let a wider body
of folks feel that participation is worth the effort.

Many folks (myself included) cannot reliably ascend, so we felt that having
asension as a prerequisite _everything_ would make participation much less
attractive to all but the most skilled core.

> If you're going to have 13 awards that distinguish between the roles, it's 
> far more meaningful to give them out based on fastest ascension time[1].
> No, 
> it isn't a perfect system.  Early wishes and such give a big advantage to
> the 
> players that get them.  It wouldn't be nethack without some element of
> luck, 
> but it is still mostly skill-based.  Furthermore, ranking by ascension time 
> makes the differences between the roles meaningful, as the harder roles
> will 
> probably take more turns.
..

That's not a bad idea, but as I said we don't want to make ascension a
pre-requisite for everything.

I can see having both sets, certainly.

